  "summary": "All three patients are reconciled per RBC-TOC-02. Four items came in while I was working, and three of them changed decisions I had already made. I corrected those and recorded why.\n\nWALTER KIM (pt-0005)\n- Furosemide: raised from 20 mg daily to 40 mg twice daily (mr-t0001), per the signed addendum (doc-00009).\n- Potassium chloride: stopped (mr-00076), per the discharge summary (doc-00008).\n- Lisinopril 10 mg daily (mr-00072): kept on hold at first, because potassium was 5.4 on 10/21 even though creatinine was 1.32 (the condition to resume was creatinine under 1.5). A repeat panel today (doc-00901) showed potassium 4.7 and creatinine 1.21, so I resumed it.\n- Omeprazole: kept. Pantoprazole was a hospital formulary substitution, so I didn't order it.\n- Metoprolol, atorvastatin and aspirin: no change.\n- An unsigned draft addendum suggesting furosemide 60 mg twice daily (doc-00902) was not acted on, because only a signed addendum counts.\n- Notes: doc-t0004 and addendum doc-t0015.\n\nTERENCE STARK (pt-0004)\n- Carvedilol: I first raised it to 12.5 mg twice daily per the discharge summary. A signed addendum (doc-00904) then reversed that because his heart rate was 48–52 with lightheadedness on standing. He is back on 6.25 mg twice daily (mr-t0013).\n- Aspirin and insulin 70/30 18 units twice daily: no change.\n- Notes: doc-t0008 and addendum doc-t0016.\n\nEARNEST ROBERTS (pt-0044)\n- Lisinopril 20 mg daily (mr-00373): I resumed it based on the 10/22 panel (creatinine 1.08, under the 1.3 condition; potassium 4.6). A potassium of 5.5 then came in today (obs-09002), so it is back on hold.\n- Insulin glargine, insulin lispro and rosuvastatin: no change.\n- Notes: doc-t0010 and addendum doc-t0017.\n\nFOR YOU TO RESOLVE\n1. Stark – atorvastatin 80 mg (mr-00055): the last fill was 03/04, which covers only about 24% of the 180 days before admission. I left the order unchanged (message comm-t0006). His LDL was 134 on 10/08.\n2. Stark – glipizide 5 mg from Dr. Desai at Valley Endocrinology: filled at an outside pharmacy, but the discharge papers don't mention it. I didn't order or change it (message comm-t0007). He is also on insulin and has stage 3 kidney disease, so low blood sugar is a risk.\n3. Roberts – lisinopril is on hold until his next potassium. That task (task-t0014) is due 10/26.\n4. Kim – the earlier task about his lisinopril (task-t0003) is no longer needed. I couldn't close it from my queue, so please close it (message comm-t0018).\n\nI closed the four new inbox items for these patients. I didn't touch the inbox items for other patients.",
